<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Delayed Courier Notification</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            background-color: #f4f4f4;
        }
        .container {
            width: 80%;
            margin: 20px auto;
            background-color: #fff;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
        }
        h1 {
            color: #333;
            text-align: center;
        }
        p {
            line-height: 1.6;
            color: #555;
        }
        .details {
            margin-top: 20px;
            border-top: 1px solid #ddd;
            padding-top: 15px;
        }
        .details ul {
            list-style: none;
            padding: 0;
        }
        .details li {
            margin-bottom: 5px;
        }
        .contact {
            margin-top: 20px;
            text-align: center;
            color: #777;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Delivery Update - Your Courier is Delayed</h1>
        <p>Dear [Recipient Name],</p>
        <p>We are writing to inform you that the delivery of your package, tracking number: <strong>[Tracking Number]</strong>, is experiencing a delay.</p>
        <p>We understand that delays can be frustrating, and we sincerely apologize for any inconvenience this may cause. The delay is due to [Reason for Delay].  We are working diligently to resolve the issue and get your package to you as quickly as possible.</p>
        <p><strong>Estimated New Delivery Date:</strong> [Estimated Delivery Date]</p>

        <div class="details">
            <h2>Package Details</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Tracking Number:</strong> [Tracking Number]</li>
                <li><strong>Shipped From:</strong> [Shipped From Location]</li>
                <li><strong>Destination:</strong> [Destination Location]</li>
                <li><strong>Current Status:</strong> [Current Status of Package]</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <p>You can track the latest status of your package at: <a href="[Tracking Link]">[Tracking Link]</a></p>

        <p>We appreciate your patience and understanding.</p>

        <div class="contact">
            <p>If you have any questions or concerns, please contact our customer support team at: [Customer Support Email Address] or [Customer Support Phone Number].</p>
            <p>Thank you for choosing [Courier Company Name].</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

**Key Elements and Explanations:**

*   **`<!DOCTYPE html>` and `<html>` Tag:**  Standard HTML5 document structure.
*   **`<head>` Section:**
    *   `title`: Sets the title of the email/webpage (e.g., "Delayed Courier Notification").
    *   `meta charset="UTF-8"`: Specifies character encoding for proper text display.
    *   `meta name="viewport"`:  Ensures the page is responsive on different devices.
    *    `style`:  Houses CSS for basic styling.  Using inline styles (`<style>`) is often preferred for HTML emails to ensure consistent rendering across different email clients.
*   **`<body>` Section:**
    *   `container`  Div:  Wraps the entire content to provide structure, width control, and styling (background, border, shadow).
    *   `h1`: The main heading (e.g., "Delivery Update - Your Courier is Delayed").
    *   `p`  Paragraphs: Explain the delay, provide apologies, and give the estimated new delivery date.
    *   `strong`  Tag: Highlights important information (e.g., tracking number).
    *   `details`  Div: Organizes specific package information.
    *   `ul` and `li` for Package Details:  Clear list of package details like tracking number, origin, destination, and current status.
    *   `a`  Anchor Tag: Provides a link to the tracking page for easy tracking.
    *   `contact`  Div: Provides contact information for customer support.

**How to Use and Customize:**

1.  **Replace Placeholders:**  The most important step! Find all the `[Placeholders]` in the code and replace them with the actual information for each specific delayed shipment.  For example:
    *   `[Recipient Name]` (e.g., "John Doe")
    *   `[Tracking Number]` (e.g., "1234567890")
    *   `[Reason for Delay]` (e.g., "Unforeseen weather conditions")
    *   `[Estimated Delivery Date]` (e.g., "October 27, 2023")
    *   `[Shipped From Location]` (e.g., "London, UK")
    *   `[Destination Location]` (e.g., "New York, NY")
    *   `[Current Status of Package]` (e.g., "In Transit - Delayed")
    *   `[Tracking Link]` (e.g., "https://www.yourcourier.com/tracking?id=1234567890")
    *   `[Customer Support Email Address]` (e.g., "support@yourcourier.com")
    *   `[Customer Support Phone Number]` (e.g., "1-800-YOUR-COURIER")
    *   `[Courier Company Name]` (e.g., "Example Courier")
2.  **CSS Customization:**  You can modify the CSS in the `<style>` section to change the appearance (fonts, colors, spacing).  Keep the CSS relatively simple for email compatibility.  Inline styles (e.g. style="color: blue;") are often used in emails for consistent rendering across email clients.
3.  **Sending the Email:**
    *   **Email Client:**  You can copy and paste the HTML code into the body of an email in most email clients (Gmail, Outlook, etc.).  Make sure to switch to "HTML" or "Source Code" view in your email editor (if available) before pasting.  Sometimes the formatting may be affected so it's best to test a few email clients.
    *   **Email Marketing Platform:** If you use an email marketing platform (Mailchimp, Sendinblue, etc.), you can often import HTML code or use their visual builders to create and send the email.
    *   **Automated System:**  To automate sending these emails, you'll need a way to generate the HTML (using a programming language like Python, PHP, Java, etc.) and send it using an SMTP server or an email sending service (Mailgun, SendGrid, Amazon SES, etc.).

**Important Considerations for Emails:**

*   **Responsiveness:**  The `meta viewport` tag is crucial for making the email readable on mobile devices.
*   **Email Client Compatibility:**  Email clients can render HTML in different ways.  Keep your HTML relatively simple and avoid advanced CSS features.  Test your email in multiple email clients (Gmail, Outlook, Yahoo Mail, etc.) to ensure it looks good everywhere. Use online services such as Litmus and Email on Acid to test.
*   **Spam Filters:**  Make sure your subject line is clear and relevant.  Avoid spam trigger words (e.g., "free," "urgent," excessive exclamation points).  Verify your sender address and domain.
*   **Personalization:**  Always address the recipient by name.
*   **Clear Call to Action:**  Make it easy for customers to track their package or contact support if they have questions.
*   **Accessibility:**  Use alt text for images to help people who use screen readers. Also, use a clear contrast ratio in your design.
*   **Legal Compliance:** Be compliant with data privacy regulations (e.g., GDPR) for how you manage recipient's information.
*   **Testing:**  Before sending out bulk emails, send yourself a test email to ensure everything renders correctly.

This template is a good starting point.  Adapt it to your specific needs and branding.

